---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "How Long Do Treadmills Last? (2026): Real Lifespans by Tier";
const description =
  "Realistic treadmill lifespans by price tier, what actually fails first, the repair-or-replace arithmetic, and why maintained machines last roughly twice as long.";

const TIERS = [
  {
    tier: "Budget ($400-$800)",
    maintained: "5-8 years",
    neglected: "2-4 years",
    firstFail: "Deck and belt, then the controller board",
    note: "Lighter frames and smaller motors leave no margin for a dry deck.",
  },
  {
    tier: "Mid-range ($800-$1,500)",
    maintained: "8-12 years",
    neglected: "4-6 years",
    firstFail: "Belt, then incline motor, then controller",
    note: "The tier where maintenance makes the biggest difference to outcome.",
  },
  {
    tier: "Premium ($1,500-$2,500)",
    maintained: "10-15 years",
    neglected: "5-8 years",
    firstFail: "Console and electronics before anything mechanical",
    note: "Frames outlive their touchscreens, which is a real obsolescence risk.",
  },
  {
    tier: "Commercial ($2,500+)",
    maintained: "15-20 years",
    neglected: "8-12 years",
    firstFail: "Belt and deck as routine consumables",
    note: "Built to be repaired rather than replaced. Parts stay available.",
  },
  {
    tier: "Walking pads ($250-$600)",
    maintained: "3-5 years",
    neglected: "2-3 years",
    firstFail: "Motor, from sustained high duty cycle",
    note: "Small motors running four hours a day are the limiting factor.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "How Long Do Treadmills Last?", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does a treadmill last on average?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Eight to twelve years for a maintained mid-range home treadmill, and four to six for a neglected one. Budget machines run five to eight years maintained; commercial-grade frames reach fifteen to twenty. The single largest variable is not price but whether the deck has been lubricated, which roughly doubles the outcome at every tier.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What usually breaks first on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The belt and deck wear first as normal consumables. After that it depends on the tier: on budget and mid-range machines the controller board or motor is next, usually as a downstream consequence of running hot on a dry deck. On premium machines the console and its software typically become the problem before anything mechanical does.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it worth repairing a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Belts, rollers, consoles and incline motors are usually worth replacing. A main motor or controller board on a mid-range machine past five years often costs a third of a better new treadmill, which tips the decision toward replacement. On commercial frames the arithmetic reverses, because the frame and motor have another decade in them.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many miles will a treadmill last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Belts are commonly rated in the region of a few thousand miles and decks somewhat longer, but mileage is a poor predictor for home machines. Most home treadmills die from heat damage caused by friction rather than from wearing out, and a lightly used machine that has never been lubricated will fail sooner than a heavily used one that has.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do treadmills last longer if you only walk on them?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Per session, yes — walking loads the motor and deck far less than running. But walkers often use their machines daily and for longer, so total running hours can be higher, and the duty cycle is what wears bearings and belts. The net effect is usually modestly better than a runner's machine, provided the same lubrication schedule is kept.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a second-hand treadmill a good idea?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can be excellent value if you can inspect it, because the failure mode you are avoiding is invisible from a photograph. Lift the belt and feel the deck for dryness, check the belt edges for fraying, run it at several speeds listening for bearing noise, and ask directly when it was last lubricated. A commercial-grade frame at five years old is often a better buy than a new budget machine.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-03",
      dateModified: "2026-09-03",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-03",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-03",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill lifespan",
      "treadmill repair",
      "treadmill maintenance",
      "used treadmill",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-lifespan" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-lifespan)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Owner's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          How Long Do <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Treadmills</span> Last?
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Eight to twelve years, or four to six. The gap is not explained by
          brand or by price — it is almost entirely explained by whether anyone
          ever lubricated the deck.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#tiers" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Lifespans by Tier
          </a>
          <a href="#repair-replace" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Repair or Replace?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 3, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">Where these numbers come from.</strong>
          The ranges below are drawn from manufacturer warranty terms, owner
          reports and our own long-term testing, not from a controlled study.
          Treat them as well-founded expectations rather than measurements — the
          spread within any tier is wide, and how a machine is used and
          maintained matters more than which tier it sits in.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#tiers" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Lifespans by Price Tier</a>
          <a href="#what-fails" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What Actually Fails, in Order</a>
          <a href="#maintenance-effect" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Why Maintenance Doubles the Number</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Reading a Warranty as a Lifespan Signal</a>
          <a href="#obsolescence" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. The Software Obsolescence Problem</a>
          <a href="#repair-replace" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Repair or Replace: The Arithmetic</a>
          <a href="#buying-used" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Buying Used, and What to Inspect</a>
          <a href="#buy-for-life" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Buying for Longevity in the First Place</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Ask how long a treadmill lasts and the honest answer is a question
          back: has anyone lubricated the deck? Two identical machines bought on
          the same day diverge by a factor of two depending on the answer, and
          that single variable outweighs brand, price and how hard the machine
          has been worked.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Short Answer</h2>
          <p>
            A maintained mid-range home treadmill lasts eight to twelve years. A
            neglected one lasts four to six. Budget machines sit a tier below
            that, commercial-grade frames a tier or two above, and walking pads
            considerably below everything.
          </p>
          <p>
            What makes those ranges unusual, compared with most household
            equipment, is how much of the variation is in the owner's hands.
            Fridges and washing machines fail on a schedule you cannot influence
            much. Treadmills fail overwhelmingly from one avoidable cause, which
            means the useful question is not "how long will this last" but "what
            determines whether it lasts".
          </p>
          <p>
            That framing also changes buying advice. Paying twice as much does buy
            longevity, but not as much as free maintenance does. A well-maintained
            $900 machine will comfortably outlive a neglected $2,000 one, and the
            neglected $2,000 one will feel like the worse purchase to its owner —
            unfairly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Lifespans by Price Tier</h2>
          <p>
            "Maintained" below means lubricated every three to six months, belt
            tension and tracking checked, motor housing kept clean. "Neglected"
            means none of that, which is the default state of most home
            treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[720px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Tier</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Maintained</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Neglected</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">What Fails First</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              TIERS.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? "bg-white" : "bg-gray-50"}>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">{row.tier}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-green-700 font-bold align-top">{row.maintained}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-red-700 align-top">{row.neglected}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-500 align-top">{row.firstFail}<span class="block text-gray-400 mt-1">{row.note}</span></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The pattern worth noticing is that the ratio is roughly two to one at
          every tier. Maintenance does not add a fixed number of years; it
          multiplies whatever the machine was capable of. That is consistent with
          the failure mechanism being thermal rather than mechanical — heat
          shortens component life proportionally rather than absolutely.
        </p>

        <div id="what-fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Actually Fails, in Order</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt.</strong> A consumable, and the only part expected to
            wear out. Years on a lubricated deck; months on a dry one. Replacing
            it is routine and inexpensive relative to the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The deck board.</strong> Also consumable on many machines, and
            often reversible — a fair number of decks can be flipped to present an
            unworn face, effectively doubling their life. Worth asking about
            before assuming a worn deck is terminal.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The incline motor.</strong> Smaller than the drive motor and
            cycled constantly by programmed workouts, so it fails more often than
            people expect. Usually replaceable as a unit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The controller board.</strong> This is the one that ends
            machines. It is the component most exposed to cumulative heat, and the
            heat comes from the motor working against friction. When a four-year
            treadmill "dies suddenly", this is almost always what died, and a dry
            deck is almost always why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The drive motor.</strong> Genuinely durable when run cool.
            Motors that fail early have nearly always been running against a dry
            deck or in a dust-choked housing for years.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rollers and bearings.</strong> Long-lived unless the belt has
            been over-tightened, which loads them continuously. This is the main
            way a well-meaning owner shortens a machine's life — see our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt tension guide</a>
            for how tight is too tight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance-effect" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Why Maintenance Doubles the Number</h2>
          <p>
            The mechanism is worth understanding, because it explains why the
            effect is so large and so invisible. The belt slides across a fixed
            deck board with your weight pressing them together. Lubricant is the
            only thing keeping that interface low-friction. As it depletes,
            friction climbs.
          </p>
          <p>
            The controller responds by feeding the motor more current to hold the
            set speed, because from its perspective the belt is being slowed by a
            load. More current is more heat, in the motor windings and in the
            controller's own power components. Neither has much thermal headroom
            in a consumer machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Electronic components degrade with cumulative thermal stress. Running
            twenty degrees hotter than designed does not fail a board today; it
            takes years off it. So the owner experiences three quiet years
            followed by an abrupt failure, and concludes the machine was badly
            built. The dry deck that caused it was fixable in ten minutes for the
            price of a bottle of silicone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Dust compounds it. The motor housing draws room air across the motor
            and controller, and a dust blanket on a board is thermal insulation on
            the thing that most needs to shed heat. That is why machines on carpet
            fare worse, and why the annual housing vacuum is on the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>
            despite feeling optional.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Reading a Warranty as a Lifespan Signal</h2>
          <p>
            Warranty terms are the closest thing to a manufacturer's own estimate
            of how long each part will last, and they are more informative than
            the marketing. Read them as three separate numbers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame warranty</strong> is usually ten years to lifetime, and
            it is the cheapest promise to make — frames rarely fail. A lifetime
            frame warranty is not evidence of a durable machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parts warranty</strong> is the informative one. One year says
            the manufacturer expects the electronics to be a risk early. Three to
            five years is a genuine statement of confidence, and it correlates
            well with the machines that turn up in owner reports still running at
            ten years. This is the number to compare between models.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Labour warranty</strong> is usually one year and occasionally
            two. It matters more than it looks, because a call-out on an
            out-of-warranty repair is often a meaningful share of the total cost.
          </p>
          <p>
            One caveat that catches people: home warranties are generally void if
            the machine is used commercially, which includes offices, rental
            properties and shared building gyms. If that describes your situation,
            check before you buy — the machine may be uncovered from day one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="obsolescence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The Software Obsolescence Problem</h2>
          <p>
            Mechanical lifespan is no longer the only clock running. On
            subscription-connected machines, the console is a tablet, and tablets
            age on a technology cycle rather than an engineering one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The realistic risks are that an app stops supporting older console
            hardware, that a subscription price rises well beyond what the
            experience is worth, or that the interactive content the machine was
            sold on becomes unavailable. None of those stops the belt turning, but
            all of them can leave a perfectly good frame feeling obsolete after
            five or six years.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is a real argument for machines that work fully without a
            subscription. Sole's approach — a plain console with manual speed and
            incline, no account required — has aged better than the touchscreen
            generation of the same era, and it is a large part of why those
            machines appear so often in our recommendations. Our
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole brand guide</a>
            covers the trade-off, and the
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack guide</a>
            is candid about the other side of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical test before buying: if the subscription ended tomorrow,
            would this machine still do what you want? If the answer is no, you
            are buying a service with a treadmill attached, and its useful life is
            the service's life rather than the frame's.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Repair or Replace: The Arithmetic</h2>
          <p>
            A workable rule: if the repair costs more than half what an equivalent
            new machine would, and the treadmill is past two-thirds of its
            expected life for its tier, replace it. Otherwise repair.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Repair.</strong> Belts, rollers, consoles, safety keys,
            incline motors, deck boards. These are bolt-on parts, widely available
            for mainstream models, and cheap relative to the machine. A belt and
            deck refresh on a good frame is close to a new treadmill in feel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Usually replace.</strong> A drive motor or controller board on
            a budget or mid-range machine past five years. Either repair commonly
            lands near a third of the price of a better new treadmill, and on a
            machine that has been running hot for years the other component is
            frequently next in line. Paying twice is the trap here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The exception.</strong> Commercial-grade frames invert the
            rule. A controller replacement on a Life Fitness or Matrix machine is
            usually good value, because the frame, deck and motor genuinely have
            another decade in them and parts stay available. These machines are
            designed to be repaired, and the second-hand market reflects that.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you land on replacement, the fault that killed the last machine is
            worth designing out rather than repeating: start from our
            <a href="/best-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">current top picks</a>
            or, if the machine mainly gets walked on, the
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Buying Used, and What to Inspect</h2>
          <p>
            Second-hand treadmills can be outstanding value precisely because the
            thing that determines their remaining life is invisible in a listing
            photograph. A five-year-old commercial frame that has been maintained
            is a better machine than a new budget treadmill at the same money.
            A five-year-old one that has not been is scrap.
          </p>
          <p>
            Inspect in this order. <strong>Lift the belt</strong> at the centre of
            the deck and feel the surface underneath — slick means maintained,
            bare and dry means the electronics have been cooking. This is the
            single most informative check and takes ten seconds.
            <strong>Check the belt edges</strong> for fraying, which indicates it
            has been running off-centre. <strong>Run it</strong> at several
            speeds and listen at both ends for bearing noise.
            <strong>Cycle the incline</strong> through its full range, since
            incline motors are a common failure. And <strong>ask directly</strong>
            when it was last lubricated — the answer, and how confidently it
            comes, tells you most of what you need.
          </p>
          <p>
            Factor in logistics before agreeing a price. Treadmills are heavy and
            awkward, most need two people and a van, and some will not fit through
            a standard doorway without partial disassembly. A bargain that needs a
            professional move is not always a bargain.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, check parts availability for the model before buying anything
            more than a few years old. A machine from a brand that has left the
            market may be unrepairable regardless of condition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buy-for-life" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Buying for Longevity in the First Place</h2>
          <p>
            If you want a machine that lasts, the signals worth weighting are not
            the ones on the front of the box.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parts warranty length</strong> over frame warranty length, for
            the reasons in section five. <strong>Weight capacity</strong> as a
            proxy for frame gauge — a machine with margin flexes less and holds
            its tracking. <strong>Machine weight itself</strong>, which correlates
            with the same thing. <strong>Independence from a subscription</strong>,
            so the console does not become the limiting factor. And
            <strong>parts availability</strong>, which favours established brands
            with a service network over whichever direct-to-consumer name is
            currently cheapest.
          </p>
          <p>
            What matters less than the marketing suggests: motor horsepower beyond
            about 3.0 CHP for home use, top speed, and screen size. None of those
            extends a machine's life, and the last one shortens its useful life if
            it ties you to a subscription.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then do the maintenance. It is the cheapest performance upgrade
            available and the only one that compounds: our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>
            is under an hour a year, and it is worth more to a treadmill's
            lifespan than doubling the purchase price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a treadmill last on average?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Eight to twelve years for a maintained mid-range home treadmill,
                and four to six for a neglected one. Budget machines run five to
                eight years maintained; commercial-grade frames reach fifteen to
                twenty. The single largest variable is not price but whether the
                deck has been lubricated, which roughly doubles the outcome at
                every tier.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What usually breaks first on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The belt and deck wear first as normal consumables. After that it
                depends on the tier: on budget and mid-range machines the
                controller board or motor is next, usually as a downstream
                consequence of running hot on a dry deck. On premium machines the
                console and its software typically become the problem before
                anything mechanical does.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth repairing a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Belts, rollers, consoles and incline motors are usually worth
                replacing. A main motor or controller board on a mid-range machine
                past five years often costs a third of a better new treadmill,
                which tips the decision toward replacement. On commercial frames
                the arithmetic reverses, because the frame and motor have another
                decade in them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many miles will a treadmill last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Belts are commonly rated in the region of a few thousand miles and
                decks somewhat longer, but mileage is a poor predictor for home
                machines. Most home treadmills die from heat damage caused by
                friction rather than from wearing out, and a lightly used machine
                that has never been lubricated will fail sooner than a heavily
                used one that has.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do treadmills last longer if you only walk on them?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Per session, yes — walking loads the motor and deck far less than
                running. But walkers often use their machines daily and for
                longer, so total running hours can be higher, and the duty cycle
                is what wears bearings and belts. The net effect is usually
                modestly better than a runner's machine, provided the same
                lubrication schedule is kept.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a second-hand treadmill a good idea?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be excellent value if you can inspect it, because the
                failure mode you are avoiding is invisible from a photograph. Lift
                the belt and feel the deck for dryness, check the belt edges for
                fraying, run it at several speeds listening for bearing noise, and
                ask directly when it was last lubricated. A commercial-grade frame
                at five years old is often a better buy than a new budget machine.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes That Shorten a Treadmill's Life</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Three of these four are things owners actively do. The last is a buying decision made years earlier.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a lifetime frame warranty means a durable machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Frames almost never fail, so a lifetime frame warranty is the cheapest promise a manufacturer can make. The informative number is the parts warranty: one year signals low confidence in the electronics, three to five is a genuine statement. Compare that figure between models and ignore the headline frame term.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying twice as much machine instead of maintaining the one you have</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Price buys longevity, but not nearly as much as free maintenance does. A well-maintained $900 treadmill will outlive a neglected $2,000 one comfortably. If the budget is a choice between a better machine and no maintenance habit, the cheaper machine with the habit is the better outcome.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Repairing a mid-range machine twice</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">When a controller board fails on a five-year-old machine that has been running hot, the motor has been through the same years of thermal stress and is often next. Paying a third of a new treadmill's price for the first repair and then facing the second is the common trap. Past two-thirds of expected life, weigh replacement seriously.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the subscription clock when buying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A frame can have fifteen years in it while its console has five, and an app that drops support for older hardware ends a machine's useful life without anything breaking. Before buying, ask whether the treadmill still does what you want if the subscription ended tomorrow. If not, you are buying a service with a treadmill attached.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

